00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.300
MAX SALTONSTALL: Today
we're here with Cristina,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.603
CEO and founder of Pandium.

00:00:07.603 --> 00:00:09.270
And we're really
excited to explore more

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.740
about what Pandium does.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.600
Before we jump
into it, Cristina,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.420
can you tell us, at its
core, what is Pandium doing?

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.670
CRISTINA FLASCHEN: Absolutely.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.570
So Pandium is an
integration platform

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.120
that makes it easier for
every B2B software company

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.920
to have their own
in-app marketplace

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.560
and also to be present in the
marketplaces of their larger

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.430
partners.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.090
So application integration
is not what it used to be,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.009
and integration is
really about distribution

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.970
and reaching your customers
wherever they are,

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.720
whether that's within
your own application

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.100
or in the marketplace
of your larger partners.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.720
Pandium is an
infrastructure platform

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.512
that makes it easier
for software companies

00:00:41.512 --> 00:00:43.179
to build and support
those marketplaces.

00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:44.929
MAX SALTONSTALL: So
it sounds like Pandium

00:00:44.929 --> 00:00:47.160
is helping businesses reach
other businesses pretty

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.085
broadly.

00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:49.710
Can you tell us a
little bit about some

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.120
of those specific companies
and how you've helped them?

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:52.990
CRISTINA FLASCHEN:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.615
We work across all
different verticals,

00:00:54.615 --> 00:00:57.840
but one of our customers is in
the sports management space.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.930
Their customers are interested
in moving information

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.512
about their rosters, their
invoices, their registrations

00:01:04.512 --> 00:01:06.720
into all of the other tools
that they use day to day,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.510
whether that's an accounting
software, a CRM, email

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.120
marketing, or sports
endemic tools that

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.650
are specific to their industry.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.420
We power their
integration marketplace

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.360
so that their customers can go
and connect to all of the tools

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.603
that they use directly from
within that application.

00:01:20.603 --> 00:01:22.020
MAX SALTONSTALL:
Pandium's helping

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.290
reduce some of the complexity
for them and their developers.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.320
How are you able to make
the developers' lives easier

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.290
as they're looking to do all
these third-party integrations?

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.540
CRISTINA FLASCHEN: Absolutely.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.360
So we are a SaaS platform, and
we help our engineering users

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.707
build integration
scripts in languages

00:01:38.707 --> 00:01:40.290
that make sense to
them without having

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.690
to deal with a lot of
the operational overhead

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.860
of the marketplace itself,
system authentication, logging,

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.600
enabling their
customer support team

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.700
to help those customers
if they have issues.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.150
We've productized everything
around that exercise

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.040
so that the engineers
at our customers

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.140
can focus on building
out the business logic

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.030
and not focus on all
that infrastructure.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:00.620
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's awesome.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.010
And it sounds like you're
going to make them happier

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.780
because they're doing
less of the overhead

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.760
and more of the
actual feature design.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.020
CRISTINA FLASCHEN:
That's the goal, yes.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.560
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's great.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.530
So can you tell me about some
of the Google technologies

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.910
that enabled you
to build Pandium

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.590
and how you put it all
together under the hood?

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.715
CRISTINA FLASCHEN: We are
an infrastructure product

00:02:15.715 --> 00:02:17.340
at our core with
some cool features

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.487
and skins on top of it.

00:02:18.487 --> 00:02:20.070
And we face a lot
of the same problems

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.110
that any developer
platform would face around

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.360
authentication,
security, and storage.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.520
We have also the
added complexity

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.870
of running third-party code.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.420
So our engineering users
at our customer sites

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.083
actually push code
to our platform.

00:02:32.083 --> 00:02:33.750
So it was really
important to us to have

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.720
an architecture that
allowed us to do this safely

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:37.920
and securely at scale.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.420
It was an obvious
choice for us when

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.400
we learned about
Kubernetes to go down

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.320
the path of microservices.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.760
It really allows us to partition
our users and our accounts

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.153
in a safe way so that if
code is doing something

00:02:50.153 --> 00:02:51.570
that we don't need
it to, we don't

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.310
have an issue like
a monolithic design

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.102
where it could take
down our entire system.

00:02:55.102 --> 00:02:58.770
And GCP includes a lot of
the kind of universal tools

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.980
that we need to support
Kubernetes at massive scale.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.140
We use Google's key
management service

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.540
to manage our authentication
into our accounts.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.780
And we also use GKE, the
managed Kubernetes engine,

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.630
to take care of a lot of the
operational and DevOps overhead

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.480
so we can focus on
building the platform.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.730
MAX SALTONSTALL: So you've
got a lot of customers each

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.530
with their own Kubernetes
clusters and instances.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.950
How do you handle
the operational load

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:23.880
of that growth?

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.422
CRISTINA FLASCHEN:
Another great part

00:03:25.422 --> 00:03:30.510
of using GKE and GCP is that
we have a great team OF SREs

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.740
behind us that can help us.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.590
It's very, very
uncommon that we have

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.090
an issue with our
production account

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.715
and have to make a
ticket with support.

00:03:36.715 --> 00:03:38.660
But if we do, we
know that we're going

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.770
to get great support,
better support than we've

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.290
gotten when we've used
other providers in the past.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.380
And we have just a massive
team behind us making sure

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.240
that all of our clusters
are running well.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.420
That is a resource that
is so valuable to us,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:52.770
especially being a growing team.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.960
In addition to that,
your docs are amazing.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.940
We really appreciate that the
language in the documentation

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.490
is not buzz-wordy.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.740
It makes it a lot easier
for us to talk not only

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.590
internally, but also with our
developer users and our product

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.650
users about our
technology when there

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.940
is common nomenclature that
makes sense for everyone

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.818
and isn't specific
to one company.

00:04:15.818 --> 00:04:18.360
MAX SALTONSTALL: I'm guessing
you have a lot of repeated work

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.829
